menu "espix filesystem"

    config ESPIX_FS_EXFAT
        bool "exFAT support in the FAT driver"
        default n
        help
            FatFs reads and writes exFAT as well as FAT12/16/32, but ESP-IDF
            ships it off: ffconf.h hard-codes FF_FS_EXFAT to 0 and exposes no
            Kconfig for it, unlike the options beside it. Enabling this patches
            that line to follow this symbol, so it is a build choice rather
            than an edit to the IDF tree.

            It also turns on FF_LBA64, which is not optional for the volumes
            exFAT is wanted for: ff.c refuses any volume whose last LBA does not
            fit in 32 bits, which at a 512-byte sector is 2TiB. Without it an
            exFAT partition larger than that mounts as FR_NO_FILESYSTEM.

            Two more 32-bit fields had to widen with it, both in the IDF tree
            and both silent when left alone: the diskio read/write function
            pointers in diskio_impl.h, and the sector parameters and ioctl
            buffers in diskio_bdl.c. See tools/patch-fatfs.py.

            With it on, `mount` accepts a volume lsblk names exfat and lsblk
            stops marking that type unsupported -- the driver is there, so the
            marker would be a lie.

            The cost, measured on the S3 build with idf.py size -- this option n
            against the same tree with it y, so the delta is the whole feature
            rather than one patch of it:

              total image   1347280 -> 1354252    +6972 bytes
              .bss            59528 -> 59528      no change
              .data           26503 -> 26503      no change

            The earlier figure for FF_FS_EXFAT alone was +5644; the rest is
            FF_LBA64. Nothing shows in .bss because a FATFS work area is
            allocated rather than static, so the RAM cost sits on the heap at
            mount. Expect a 3TB volume to allocate a few KB more there and to
            spend a moment reading its allocation bitmap the first time free
            space is asked for.

            Licensing: exFAT is covered by Microsoft patents, and FatFs's author
            has historically noted that a licence may be needed for commercial
            use. No patent or licence text ships with IDF's FatFs, and that claim
            has not been verified here -- check the terms before shipping this on
            by default.

    config ESPIX_FS_EXT4
        bool "ext2/3/4 support (read-only)"
        default n
        help
            Mounts ext2, ext3 and ext4 as the third filesystem, after littlefs
            and FAT/exFAT. The library is lwext4, vendored as the esp_lwext4
            component in components/esp_lwext4, and its driver is espix's own --
            components/espix_fs/ext.c, in the shape of fat.c -- so a mount made
            here goes through espix's VFS and its permission check rather than
            being reachable around it.

            Read-only. ext4_mount() takes read_only as an argument and every
            mutating operation refuses with EROFS, so the two agree by
            construction. Writes need the vendored lwext4's write-path defect
            dealt with first; see docs/ROADMAP.md.

            One thing this gets that the FAT driver cannot: stat with a real
            inode behind it. Mode, owner and timestamps come from the inode
            rather than from a mount-wide uid=/gid= rule, and df is lwext4's own
            count rather than arithmetic over a FAT. The exception is size, which
            is truncated at 4 GiB like everything else while off_t is 32 bits --
            see docs/UPSTREAM.md.

            With it on, lsblk stops marking ext2/ext3/ext4 unsupported and `mount`
            accepts it, on the same rule as exFAT: the driver is there, so the
            marker would be a lie.

            The cost, measured the way the exFAT option above was -- this option
            n against the same tree with it y, so the delta is the whole feature:

              total image   1355960 -> 1475820    +119860 bytes
              .text          968970 -> 1083550    +114580
              .rodata        259956 ->  265220      +5264
              .bss            59576 ->   70008     +10432
              .data           26519 ->   26535        +16
              DIRAM .text     83839 ->   83839          0

            The last line is the one that matters most: no IRAM, which is the
            first thing to run out here and is already at its whole 16KB. Nothing
            lwext4 needs is time-critical enough to be placed there, and its
            block cache is a heap allocation rather than a static array.

            The .bss is the largest of the rest and is mostly espix's own handle
            table -- eight open files or directories at once, each holding an
            ext4_dir of 320 bytes and a struct dirent of 260 even when it is a
            file. A union would cut about 1.4KB of it back, and has not been done
            yet.

            lwext4 carries the extents and journal code either way, because
            CONFIG_LWEXT4_FEATURE_SET_EXT4 is what a Linux volume needs -- an
            ext4 filesystem has the extents feature and the ext2 or ext3 set
            would refuse it.

endmenu

    config ESPIX_FS_EXT4_FAIL_WRITE_ABOVE
        int "Refuse the first block write at or above this address (0 = off)"
        depends on ESPIX_FS_EXT4
        default 0
        help
            Injects one I/O failure into the block device under a mounted ext
            volume: the first write at or above this byte address is refused,
            once, which is what a medium that has stopped answering looks like.

            It exists because the question it answers -- whether lwext4 aborts
            its transaction and reports the failure, or commits over it -- cannot
            be asked any other way, and because a passing e2fsck is not evidence
            about it.

            An address rather than a count of writes, because a count cannot
            distinguish a failed metadata write from a failed file data write --
            and lwext4 simply reissues the former, so a test aimed at a count
            proves nothing. On a volume laid out like the one this was written
            against, metadata sits low (0x400, 0x1000) and data high (0x8800...),
            so 0x80000000 aims at a data write. Zero, the default, is off.

